course: politics
lesson: 39
title: Arguing politely and never calling names
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: An argument means giving reasons for your view, not a fight: you can disagree with an idea and still be friends with the person who has it.
explain: Think of two mates arguing over which pizza to order: "pineapple's wrong because..." keeps it fun, but "you're stupid" ends dinner, because the moment you insult someone they stop listening and start defending.
explain: Strong arguers steelman: they say the other idea back in its best, strongest words before answering it, and they look for common ground, what both sides want.
explain: Saying "I don't know yet" and changing your mind after learning more are signs of a thinking person, not a weak one.
explain: Families vote different ways for many reasons, a vote can stay private, and calling a whole group "stupid" ignores all those reasons and the nice people in it.
fact: Disagreeing about ideas is fine; calling an idea "stupid" makes the other person feel attacked, while "I don't agree, because..." keeps the talk going.
fact: Calling people names because of their political views is hurtful and does not change minds; asking "why?" works better.
fact: Each party usually thinks it is helping the country, just in different ways, so people often hear only the worst version of the other side.
fact: Steelmanning means saying the other person's idea in its strongest, fairest way; strawmanning means making it sound silly so it is easy to knock down.
fact: Saying "I don't know yet, I need to learn more" is honest, and it is fine to wait, read and listen before deciding.
fact: Changing your mind after learning something new is a sign of a thinking person; "flip-flopper" is an unkind word for changing views often.
fact: In many countries voting is secret, and nobody has to say how they or their family voted.
fact: When people shout over each other nobody learns anything; good debates let each person finish, then reply.
fact: Online arguments heat up fast because people cannot see faces; you can say "I disagree" once, walk away, block, or tell a grown-up if it gets nasty.
fact: Finding common ground means starting from what both sides want, and a compromise is a deal where each side gives up a bit.
fact: A stereotype is the unfair idea that everyone in a group is the same; people with different views can be kind, clever and caring.
fact: Words in this lesson: "don't have a cow" means calm down; "bruv" in London means mate; "tamping" in Wales means furious; "cwtch" in Wales means a cuddle; "can or not" in Singapore English means "is that OK?"
exercise: Jake says Liam's four-day-week idea is "stupid". What would keep the talk friendly? | Saying "I don't agree, because..." | Saying it louder | Walking off without a word | Calling Liam's other ideas stupid too
exercise: What is an argument, in the good sense? | Giving reasons for your view | A fight with shouting | Calling the other person names | Refusing to listen to anyone
exercise: A boy calls another boy's family "idiots" because of how they voted. What is true? | Name-calling hurts and does not change minds | Name-calling is the best way to win a debate | Families all vote the same way | It is fine if you really disagree
exercise: Grace's friend says her mum's party "wants to ruin the country". What could Grace say? | "I think they want to help in a different way. What does your dad worry about?" | "Your dad's party is even worse." | "My mum's party is perfect, has never once been wrong, and your dad should vote for it." | "I'm never talking to you again."
exercise: What is steelmanning? | Saying the other person's idea in its strongest, fairest way | Building a strong wall of steel around your own idea so nobody can touch it | Making the other person's idea sound as silly as you can before you answer | Shouting your own view over and over until the other person gives up
exercise: What is strawmanning? | Making the other idea sound silly so it is easy to knock down | Saying the other idea in its best, fairest words before you reply to it | Agreeing with everything the other person says | Asking the other person for their reasons
exercise: Finn is asked about zoos and says, "I don't know yet, I need to learn more." Is that OK? | Yes, it is honest to wait before deciding | No, you must always pick a side at once | No, it means he is not clever | No, it is rude to the teacher
exercise: Hannah changes her mind about uniforms after hearing a good reason. What does that show? | She is a thinking person who learns | She is weak and easily fooled by anyone who talks | She was lying about what she thought before | She did not really listen to the reason at all
exercise: Kai's friend asks who his family voted for and Kai feels uncomfortable. What can he say? | "That's private in my family." | "Tell me yours first or I'll tell everyone." | "We voted for the best party, obviously." | "Your family voted wrong."
exercise: Two grown-ups on TV shout over each other. What makes a better debate? | Letting each person finish, then replying | Shouting louder than the other person | Turning off both microphones forever | Letting only the presenter speak
exercise: Someone online swears at Dylan about video game prices. What is a good choice? | Say "I disagree" once, walk away or block | Swear back even louder so they know you mean it | Keep arguing all night | Send rude messages to their friends
exercise: Amira and Mateo argue about a class hamster. What do they agree on? | They both want the hamster to be happy | They both want no hamster in the class at all | They both think the other one is being stupid | They both want to take two hamsters home
exercise: What is a compromise? | A deal where each side gives up a bit | One side winning everything | Refusing to talk any more | Calling the other side names
exercise: Chloe's uncle says anyone who votes for a party is stupid, but her friend's parents voted that way and they are nice. What is the uncle using? | A stereotype that everyone in a group is the same | A steelman, saying the other side's best reasons fairly | A fair fact-check | A secret ballot
exercise: Why do online arguments heat up fast? | People cannot see each other's faces | The internet makes everyone angry by law | Online people are always wrong | Phones make words louder
test: "your idea's well dumb, bruv" — what is a better way to disagree? | "I don't agree, and here's why..." | "Your face is dumb too." | "Whatever, loser." | Saying nothing and sulking for a week
test: In Cardiff: "I'm proper tamping, he called my nan's party a bunch of muppets!" What is the most helpful view? | Name-calling hurts and does not change anyone's mind | Calling names back is the only way to make him stop it | His nan should change her party | Muppets are a real political party
test: What would happen if both sides of a class argument had to say the other side's best reason first? | They would understand each other and argue more fairly | They would forget their own ideas | The teacher would have to pick a winner | Nobody would ever agree to anything
test: "don't have a cow, it's just a hamster" — the twins keep repeating themselves louder. What is the teacher's best question? | What do you both agree on? | Who can shout the loudest? | Who is the stupidest here? | Why don't you both give up?
test: A boy says, "If you change your mind, you're a loser." What is the better view? | Changing your mind after learning is a sign of thinking | He is right, a real winner never changes his mind about anything | Only grown-ups are allowed to change their minds, not children | People who change their minds were lying about what they thought
test: In Singapore: "You tell me who your parents vote for, can or not?" What is a polite answer? | "That's private in my family, sorry." | "Only if you give me your snack." | "They voted for the clever party." | "Your parents voted for the wrong one."
test: Dylan is about to swear back at a stranger online. What would be wiser? | Leave it, block if needed, and tell a grown-up if it gets nasty | Find the stranger's school and post about them | Swear in every comment they make | Make a fake account to annoy them
test: In a cartoon, the cat says the dog wants "to eat all the food in the world", but the dog only said he was hungry. What trick is that? | A strawman | A steelman | A compromise | A survey
test: "Anyone who votes for that lot is thick" — what is the fairer view? | People vote for many reasons and can be kind and clever | Everyone who votes that way is exactly the same kind of person | Only stupid people vote at all | Voting shows how clever you are
test: "Have you no opinion? Just pick a side, quick!" Finn hasn't learned about zoos yet. What is best? | Say he needs to learn more first | Pick a side at random | Copy whatever his best friend says | Shout louder than everyone else
test: Liam and Jake are best mates but disagree about a four-day week. Can they stay friends? | Yes, you can disagree about ideas and still be friends | No, friends must agree about everything | No, one must admit the other is stupid | Only if one of them changes school
test: After a row, Amira gives her friend a cwtch and says, "We both want what's best for the hamster." What has she found? | Common ground | A stereotype | A strawman | A secret ballot
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises an argument as understood in this lesson? | The advancement of reasons in support of a view | A physical altercation between parties | The use of insulting epithets against an opponent | The refusal to attend to opposing views
exam: Identify the practice whereby a participant restates the opposing position in its most cogent form prior to responding. | Steelmanning | Strawmanning the opponent | Seeking a compromise | Stereotyping the opponent
exam: It is given that a pupil is asked to disclose how the members of his family voted. Select the response most consistent with the established facts. | He is under no obligation to disclose it, the vote being secret | He is obliged to disclose it upon request | He is obliged to disclose it to his teacher only | He is obliged to disclose it in return for that of the enquirer
exam: Whereas a person alters his view upon receipt of new information, determine the characterisation most consistent with this lesson. | It is evidence of reasoning rather than of weakness | It is evidence of dishonesty in his former view | It is evidence that he did not attend to the information | It is permissible for adults only
